  <chapter id="ch:geometry">
    <title>Geometry</title>

    <section id="sec:triangles">
      <title>Right triangles</title>

      Geometry begins with the triangle. The most famous result about right
      triangles is due to antiquity <cite paper="pythagoras"/>.

      <theorem id="thm:pyth">
        <title>Pythagorean Theorem</title>
        For a right triangle with legs $a$ and $b$ and hypotenuse $c$,
        $$a^2 + b^2 = c^2.$$
      </theorem>

      The same idea underlies much of classical geometry <cite paper="euclid"/>.

      <subsection id="sub:converse">
        <title>The converse</title>
        The converse holds too: if $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right.
      </subsection>
    </section>
  </chapter>

  <!-- The project's single references list lives here; it lists every paper cited
       from any file, in citation order. -->
  <bibliography src="refs.ref"/>
